## Batch processing

```sh
# process all JPEGs in the current directory
exiftool -Artist="Jane Smith" *.jpg

# process recursively
exiftool -r -Artist="Jane Smith" /path/to/photos/

# overwrite originals instead of creating _original backups
exiftool -overwrite_original -Artist="Jane Smith" *.jpg
```

> By default exiftool saves a backup of each modified file as `filename_original`.
> Use `-overwrite_original` to skip this — it cannot be undone.

---

## Remove all metadata

```sh
exiftool -all= image.jpg                 # strip everything
exiftool -all= -overwrite_original *.jpg # strip all, no backups
```

---

## Gotchas

**Backups** — exiftool creates a `filename_original` backup by default. Use
`-overwrite_original` intentionally, not as a reflex.

**Multiple groups** — Description, Author, and Keywords each live in several
metadata groups (EXIF, IPTC, XMP). A tag written to one group may not be seen
by apps that read from another. When in doubt, write to both EXIF and XMP.

**`-Keywords+` vs `-Keywords`** — the bare form replaces the entire list; the
`+` form appends. Easy to accidentally delete existing keywords.

**GPS Ref tags** — `GPSLatitudeRef` and `GPSLongitudeRef` are required for
correct parsing. Setting the coordinate without the Ref can produce ambiguous
or incorrect results in some apps.

**Encoding** — if your values include accented characters, add
`-charset iptc=UTF8` to avoid IPTC encoding errors.

